WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.600
Hello and welcome to this unusual AI Coffee&nbsp;
Break. Today we’ll give you a teaser about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:13.740
some of our own work, which was accepted at ACL&nbsp;
2023. We will present it in July in Toronto, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:19.140
if you happen to be at ACL in Toronto this&nbsp;
year, do not hesitate to say hi! So here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.040
we’ll leave you with the bit we&nbsp;
have recorded to present our paper.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:31.440
Hello! We are happy to present „MM-SHAP” a&nbsp;
reliable way to measure relative contributions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:39.060
of individual modalities in multimodal models. Why&nbsp;
is measuring multimodal contributions important?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:46.440
Vision and language models have better and better&nbsp;
performance across all possible VL tasks. But can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:52.140
we trust they do this for the right reasons?&nbsp;
Fundamentally, these models: are transformers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.120
that work on text and image input. They are&nbsp;
trained, for example, with the image-sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:04.740
alignment objective to say whether a pair of text&nbsp;
and images match or mismatch. Now, there is reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:11.700
to believe that not all modalities matter for&nbsp;
these models equally, since they can suffer from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:18.000
unimodal collapse. This means, even if trained&nbsp;
multimodally, a model can exploit one modality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.840
far more than the other, and indeed we observe&nbsp;
that on multimodal tasks, unimodal baselines can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:31.080
be close in accuracy to multimodal models. The&nbsp;
reason are often dataset biases: for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.140
“how many” questions can be confidently answered&nbsp;
with “two” if “two” is the most frequent answer in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:43.260
the dataset. Or if questions about existence of an&nbsp;
object can be typically answered with “yes”, due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:50.040
to so-called plausibility bias. Previous work has&nbsp;
used model performance to assess a model’s focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:56.160
on a modality, for example by testing whether it&nbsp;
is sensitive to changing matching image-caption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.980
pairs into mismatching ones, or by measuring&nbsp;
whether their accuracy changes when exchanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:09.960
existing images with random ones and questions for&nbsp;
VQA with random questions. Or by deleting image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:16.560
regions or text tokens and measuring whether model&nbsp;
performance changes. But accuracy-based measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:23.460
are not ideal. For example, the visual importance&nbsp;
considers the difference in model accuracy when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:30.660
uses both vision and text and with missing visual&nbsp;
information. This is problematic for wrong model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:36.540
predictions, since in these cases the first term&nbsp;
is 0 and we expect the second term to be zero too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:42.780
and this results in a visual importance of zero.&nbsp;
But the model may well have relied on the visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:49.260
modality, but incorrectly. So, we propose a&nbsp;
performance-agnostic measure of multimodal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:55.560
contributions. First, we rely on Shapley Values&nbsp;
from game theory. Shapley values compute a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:01.080
fair pay-out for each player based on their&nbsp;
contribution to a game’s outcome. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:07.500
if machine learning were a soccer game, we would&nbsp;
first asses the base value, for the game outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.900
when no player is playing. Then we gradually let&nbsp;
them play and measure in all possible combinations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:18.960
of teams from our available players, what the game&nbsp;
outcomes are and we determine how much payment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.240
they deserve. For transformers, the players&nbsp;
are tokens and they receive pay-outs from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.520
contribution towards the model prediction (for&nbsp;
example the probability or activation value it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:35.160
computed for a sample) irrespective of whether&nbsp;
its prediction is correct or not. We take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.800
difference between the model output with a token&nbsp;
being active and with it inactive, and we do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.160
for all possible combinations of tokens switched&nbsp;
on and off. To get the contributions for a token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:53.820
(which can be positive or negative), we sum up&nbsp;
and normalize over all marginal contributions. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:59.460
vision and language transformers, we have image&nbsp;
tokens and text tokens and we can compute their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:05.760
contribution towards the model prediction, such as&nbsp;
the image-sentence-alignment score. And luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.400
none of the computations are accuracy-based. We&nbsp;
define MM-SHAP by calculating the contribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:17.040
percentage of text tokens and compare it to&nbsp;
the contribution percentage of image tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.160
which can be extended to multiple modalities.&nbsp;
For image-sentence alignment, we hypothesize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:29.520
image and text contribute equally on average at&nbsp;
corpus-level because it is useful when comparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:35.640
different models on the same task and data. Using&nbsp;
MM-SHAP on sample-level, we see how image and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:42.480
text tokens contribute towards the predicted 99%&nbsp;
image-sentence alignment score in blue and tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.240
that contributed against, in red. Introducing&nbsp;
an error in the caption decreases the alignment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.520
score and the token contributions reflect the&nbsp;
misalignment, with the token “keyboard” showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.520
negative contribution. At dataset- and task-level&nbsp;
we see that models such as CLIP are multimodally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.880
balanced, while ALBEF models are more textually&nbsp;
focused. This trend holds over VQA data as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.320
On the VALSE benchmark, testing models with&nbsp;
fine-grained mismatches, we see even better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:16.080
that CLIP is balanced, LXMERT has a higher visual&nbsp;
preference, while ALBEF models are more textual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:23.640
showing that different VL models behave&nbsp;
differently on the same task. Finetuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:29.220
also affects multimodal contributions and with&nbsp;
MM-SHAP, we can measure how much, even in cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:35.460
where task accuracy is very low and accuracy-based&nbsp;
metrics would fail. Throughout our analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:42.420
we consistently plotted both accuracy and MM-SHAP&nbsp;
scores, because we envision MM-SHAP to complement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:48.420
accuracy, not replace it, since we are of course&nbsp;
interested in how well models solve a task;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:54.480
but it’s only by using accuracy-agnostic metrics&nbsp;
such as MM-SHAP that we can measure how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:00.120
models rely on each modality. We invite&nbsp;
you to read our paper for more details.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:05.400
Thanks for sticking until the very end of&nbsp;
this, it’s great that someone is interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.920
in the work I do for my PhD. You know, it’s not&nbsp;
much, but it is honest work. If you liked this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.880
maybe you want to watch other videos on our&nbsp;
channel. If you like work from large companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

